1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,438
At the college fest, Aarav
touches the big stage amplifier.

2
00:00:03,738 --> 00:00:06,162
It is hot, and covered in metal fins.

3
00:00:06,462 --> 00:00:09,867
His phone also plays
music, but it stays cool.

4
00:00:10,167 --> 00:00:11,913
Where does all that heat come from?

5
00:00:11,913 --> 00:00:13,363
And how do engineers cut it?

6
00:00:13,863 --> 00:00:16,362
So far, our amplifiers
made small signals bigger.

7
00:00:16,362 --> 00:00:17,840
Those are voltage amplifiers.

8
00:00:18,190 --> 00:00:20,592
A power amplifier has a bigger job.

9
00:00:20,592 --> 00:00:23,253
It must push real power into a speaker.

10
00:00:23,603 --> 00:00:25,944
Now think of pushing a child on a swing.

11
00:00:26,294 --> 00:00:29,129
One helper pushes and
pulls the whole time.

12
00:00:29,129 --> 00:00:30,891
Smooth, but very tiring.

13
00:00:31,241 --> 00:00:32,861
Two helpers could share it.

14
00:00:32,861 --> 00:00:34,924
Each one works only half the swing.

15
00:00:35,274 --> 00:00:38,600
Or one helper gives a short
kick at the top of each swing.

16
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:41,011
The swing keeps moving by itself.

17
00:00:41,011 --> 00:00:43,638
Very little effort, but only for a swing.

18
00:00:44,288 --> 00:00:47,452
These are the three
classes of power amplifier.

19
00:00:47,452 --> 00:00:48,623
A, B and C.

20
00:00:49,273 --> 00:00:51,045
Let's see them on the breadboard.

21
00:00:51,395 --> 00:00:56,056
One transistor, a speaker load, and
a bias knob that sets the operating

22
00:00:56,056 --> 00:00:56,956
point.

23
00:00:57,027 --> 00:00:59,364
Watch the collector current on the scope.

24
00:00:59,364 --> 00:01:00,781
It is shown in violet.

25
00:01:01,131 --> 00:01:02,031
Class A.

26
00:01:01,730 --> 00:01:03,767
The operating point sits in the middle.

27
00:01:04,117 --> 00:01:07,741
The current flows for the whole
cycle, all three sixty degrees.

28
00:01:08,091 --> 00:01:10,311
The output is a faithful copy.

29
00:01:10,661 --> 00:01:13,159
But current flows even with no music.

30
00:01:13,159 --> 00:01:14,477
That wastes power.

31
00:01:15,127 --> 00:01:16,027
Class B.

32
00:01:15,845 --> 00:01:18,483
The operating point moves down to cut off.

33
00:01:18,833 --> 00:01:22,733
Now current flows only for half
the cycle, one eighty degrees.

34
00:01:23,083 --> 00:01:24,006
Less waste.

35
00:01:24,006 --> 00:01:28,500
But half the wave is missing, so
class B transistors work in pairs.

36
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:30,050
Class C.

37
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:31,756
The base is pushed below cut off.

38
00:01:32,106 --> 00:01:35,954
Current flows only in short pulses,
less than one eighty degrees.

39
00:01:36,304 --> 00:01:40,097
A tuned circuit of a coil and a
capacitor acts like the swing.

40
00:01:40,447 --> 00:01:45,095
It turns the kicks back into a smooth
wave, but only at one frequency.

41
00:01:45,745 --> 00:01:47,797
Now see all three on the load line.

42
00:01:48,147 --> 00:01:50,921
The class depends on where
the operating point sits.

43
00:01:51,271 --> 00:01:52,905
Class A sits in the middle.

44
00:01:52,905 --> 00:01:54,831
The whole input wave gets through.

45
00:01:55,181 --> 00:01:56,766
Class B sits at cut off.

46
00:01:56,766 --> 00:01:58,658
Only the top half gets through.

47
00:01:59,008 --> 00:02:00,890
Class C sits below cut off.

48
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:02,964
Only the very tips get through.

49
00:02:03,614 --> 00:02:07,729
The less time current flows, the
less power is wasted as heat.

50
00:02:08,079 --> 00:02:10,498
But the output looks less like the input.

51
00:02:10,498 --> 00:02:12,055
That is called distortion.

52
00:02:12,705 --> 00:02:17,732
There is also class AB, in between A
and B, used in many music amplifiers.

53
00:02:18,382 --> 00:02:19,827
Less time conducting.

54
00:02:19,827 --> 00:02:20,727
Less heat.

55
00:02:20,676 --> 00:02:21,819
More distortion.

56
00:02:22,619 --> 00:02:23,669
Now the maths.

57
00:02:23,669 --> 00:02:27,007
Efficiency tells how much
supply power reaches the load.

58
00:02:27,357 --> 00:02:32,947
Efficiency, eta, equals AC output
power, over DC input power, times one

59
00:02:32,947 --> 00:02:33,847
hundred.

60
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:37,483
For class A, the supply gives DC power.

61
00:02:37,483 --> 00:02:39,131
VCC times ICQ.

62
00:02:39,831 --> 00:02:43,906
The best operating point is
in the middle, at half of VCC.

63
00:02:44,606 --> 00:02:47,591
The biggest swing has
a peak current of ICQ.

64
00:02:47,941 --> 00:02:50,600
Its peak voltage is half of VCC.

65
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:55,571
AC power is peak voltage times
peak current, divided by two.

66
00:02:56,271 --> 00:03:00,253
That gives VCC times ICQ, divided by four.

67
00:03:00,953 --> 00:03:03,956
So the best class A
efficiency is one quarter.

68
00:03:03,956 --> 00:03:05,346
Twenty five percent.

69
00:03:06,046 --> 00:03:09,118
With a transformer, class
A can reach fifty percent.

70
00:03:09,468 --> 00:03:12,495
Class B can reach seventy
eight point five percent.

71
00:03:13,145 --> 00:03:14,620
Class C goes higher still.

72
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:16,747
That is why it suits radio transmitters.

73
00:03:17,397 --> 00:03:18,446
Worked example.

74
00:03:18,446 --> 00:03:20,665
Why is the stage amplifier so hot?

75
00:03:21,015 --> 00:03:24,073
It runs from twelve volts,
and draws two amperes.

76
00:03:24,423 --> 00:03:27,424
It sends six watts of
sound power to the speaker.

77
00:03:27,774 --> 00:03:30,474
DC input power is twelve times two.

78
00:03:30,474 --> 00:03:31,894
Twenty four watts.

79
00:03:32,594 --> 00:03:35,045
Efficiency is six over twenty four.

80
00:03:35,045 --> 00:03:37,071
That is twenty five percent.

81
00:03:37,771 --> 00:03:40,092
The other eighteen watts become heat.

82
00:03:40,092 --> 00:03:42,234
That is why it needs metal fins.

83
00:03:42,884 --> 00:03:44,345
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:03:44,695 --> 00:03:49,271
Many students think class C is best for
music, because it is most efficient.

85
00:03:49,621 --> 00:03:50,521
It is not.

86
00:03:50,505 --> 00:03:52,357
Its current comes in pulses.

87
00:03:52,357 --> 00:03:53,915
The distortion is huge.

88
00:03:54,565 --> 00:03:58,179
Class C needs a tuned circuit,
so it works at one frequency.

89
00:03:58,179 --> 00:04:00,035
Radio transmitters, not music.

90
00:04:00,685 --> 00:04:02,306
Where is this asked?

91
00:04:02,306 --> 00:04:04,167
In PUC board papers.

92
00:04:04,817 --> 00:04:08,702
Classify power amplifiers, compare
their efficiency and distortion, and

93
00:04:08,702 --> 00:04:09,982
find the efficiency.

94
00:04:10,632 --> 00:04:11,532
Try this.

95
00:04:11,394 --> 00:04:15,882
Collector current flows for the full
three sixty degrees of the input cycle.

96
00:04:15,882 --> 00:04:19,942
The amplifier draws zero point five
amperes from a twenty volt supply,

97
00:04:19,942 --> 00:04:22,051
and gives two watts to the load.

98
00:04:22,051 --> 00:04:24,188
Which class, and what efficiency?

99
00:04:29,588 --> 00:04:30,488
B.

100
00:04:29,922 --> 00:04:31,378
Class A, twenty percent.

101
00:04:31,728 --> 00:04:34,463
Current for the full three
sixty degrees means class A.

102
00:04:34,813 --> 00:04:38,373
DC input power is twenty
times zero point five.

103
00:04:38,373 --> 00:04:39,326
Ten watts.

104
00:04:39,676 --> 00:04:41,487
Efficiency is two over ten.

105
00:04:41,487 --> 00:04:45,252
That is twenty percent, below
the twenty five percent limit.

106
00:04:45,902 --> 00:04:47,449
Screenshot this card.

107
00:04:50,649 --> 00:04:52,776
Next, the push pull amplifier.

108
00:04:52,776 --> 00:04:55,319
How do two transistors share one wave?
